answer:
**Idea.** Share one counter and a "whose turn" rule: thread `i` (0, 1, 2) may print only when `(counter - 1) % 3 == i`. Other threads wait. After printing, a thread increments the counter and wakes the others.

**Solution with `synchronized`, `wait` and `notifyAll`.**
```java
class Printer {
    private int next = 1;
    private final int max;
    Printer(int max) { this.max = max; }

    synchronized void print(int id) throws InterruptedException {
        while (next <= max) {
            while (next <= max && (next - 1) % 3 != id) wait();
            if (next > max) break;
            System.out.println("T" + (id + 1) + ": " + next++);
            notifyAll();
        }
        notifyAll();
    }
}
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Printer p = new Printer(20);
        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            final int id = i;
            new Thread(() -> {
                try { p.print(id); } catch (InterruptedException e) { Thread.currentThread().interrupt(); }
            }).start();
        }
    }
}
```

**Why it works.**
- The monitor (`synchronized`) gives mutual exclusion on `next`, so no two threads print at once.
- `wait()` releases the monitor and sleeps until another thread calls `notifyAll()`.
- The condition is checked in a `while` loop, not an `if`, because of spurious wakeups and because with `notifyAll` a thread may be woken when it is not its turn.
- `notifyAll` (not `notify`) is needed: with `notify` you might wake a thread whose turn it is not and the right one is never signalled, so the program hangs.
- The `max` check after waking lets all threads terminate when the last number has been printed.

**Alternatives.** Three `Semaphore`s in a ring (`s0` starts with 1 permit, `s1` and `s2` with 0): thread `i` acquires its own, prints, and releases the next, which is simple and has no spurious wakeup concerns. A `ReentrantLock` with three `Condition` objects avoids waking everyone. A single-threaded executor would defeat the purpose of the exercise.
